-- title:
--   p. 6: the static chain has a unique stationary distribution
-- statement:
--   Let $q^s$ be feasible for Problem (2) and induce a strongly connected network (in the digraph of positive demand flows $\Lambda_{ij}q^s_{ij}>0$ every location reaches every location), and suppose there is at least one location. Then for every number of vehicles $K$, the continuous-time Markov chain of the static policy $q^s$ (fractions $q^s_{ij}$ at occupied locations, $0$ at empty ones) has exactly one stationary distribution $\pi$.
--
--   This is the paper's remark that under $q^s$ "the network is irreducible and belongs to the class of BCMP networks" (p. 6). It makes $R(q^s)$ and the availability $\alpha(K)$ well defined.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: trip durations i.i.d. with a general distribution with rational Laplace transform and mean 1/μij, represented by a network of exponential stages (p. 4) → a phase-type law per pair: finitely many stages, entry probabilities β (total ≤ 1; the defect is a zero-length trip), rates r between stages, exit rates e, an exit reachable from every stage; 1/μij = β(−T)⁻¹𝟙; reason: the paper's state counts vehicles at each stage of the transit queue (footnote 1), so the model exists exactly for laws with such a stage network, i.e. phase-type laws; the general (not only Coxian) form is taken
--   - encoding: demand, prices, rewards and travel data on the pairs (i,j) ∈ 𝒜 (Λij > 0) → all of them given on every pair of locations; reason: pairs with Λij = 0 enter every rate, sum and constraint with factor 0; no instance changes
--   - encoding: prices pij with acceptance fractions qij = 1 − Fij(pij) (p. 5) → the fractions q ∈ [0,1] and the per-trip rewards Iij(q) as primitives, q·Iij(q) concave on [0,1]; reason: the paper reformulates the policy in terms of the quantiles q (p. 5); concavity of q·I is its only requirement
--   - generalization: q^s an optimal solution of Problem (2) with Δ ≥ K → q^s feasible for Problem (2) (bounds, balanced flows, workload ≤ Δ), any Δ; reason: the argument uses only that the flows of q^s are balanced and q^s ∈ [0,1]; the paper's optimal q^s is a special case
--   - reading: "q^s induces a strongly connected network" (p. 6) → FlowStronglyConnected: in the digraph on all locations with an arc a → b whenever Λab q^s_ab > 0, every location reaches every location; reason: the network induced by a price vector is the graph of its positive demand flows; with one location the condition holds trivially
--   - reading: "the network is irreducible" (p. 6) → the static chain has exactly one stationary distribution; reason: transit queues without flow and stages that cannot be entered are transient, so the chain has one closed class rather than being irreducible on all states; uniqueness of π is what the paper uses
--   - encoding: N locations (N ≥ 1) → at least one location ([Nonempty P.L]); reason: with no location and K ≥ 1 there is no state; the paper has N ≥ 1 locations
--   - generalization: the network generated by 𝒩 and 𝒜 is strongly connected (p. 4, without loss of generality) → not assumed separately; reason: it follows from the strong connectivity of the flow of q^s, whose arcs lie in 𝒜; the paper's instances are a special case
-- source:
--   Benjaafar, Shen, Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks, SSRN 3998297 (version of 2023-01-10), https://ssrn.com/abstract=3998297, p. 6 (unnumbered)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_IsFeasible2
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_staticPolicy
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_IsStationary
import Definitions.Def_VehShare_SeqBounds_FlowStronglyConnected

namespace VehShare.SeqBounds

/-- **The static chain has exactly one stationary law.**
Benjaafar, Shen, *Pricing in On-Demand (and One-Way) Vehicle Sharing Networks*, SSRN 3998297
(version of 2023-01-10), p. 6 (unnumbered): "Then the network is irreducible and belongs to the class of BCMP
networks ( Baskett et al. (1975))".

Let `q^s` be feasible for Problem (2) and induce a strongly connected network. For every number of
vehicles `K`, the chain of the static policy has exactly one stationary distribution.

**Formalization Note.** Only feasibility of `q^s` (bounds and balanced flows) is assumed, with any
`Δ` (a generalization of the paper's optimal `q^s`). "Irreducible" is read as "one closed
communicating class": transit queues with no flow (`Λ_ij q^s_ij = 0`) and stages that cannot be
entered are transient, so the chain on the whole state space is not irreducible in the strict
sense, but its stationary law is unique, which is what the paper uses. At least one location is
assumed (Encoding 8: with no location and `K ≥ 1` there is no state). -/
theorem static_stationary_unique (P : Instance) [Nonempty P.L] (K : ℕ) (Δ : ℝ)
    (qs : P.L → P.L → ℝ) (hqs : IsFeasible2 P Δ qs) (hsc : FlowStronglyConnected P qs) :
    ∃! π : State P K → ℝ, IsStationary (staticPolicy P K qs hqs.1) π := by sorry

end VehShare.SeqBounds
